#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060621} :activity/id #uuid "b9e3a4de-52c9-5d8e-b674-826cfeb1cdc9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048155 :topic/id #uuid "839dcf0f-e9a0-5855-bd33-2b24510274e8" :topic/math-academy-id 249} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061001 :tutorial/content "An expression is a **tautology** if it is always true, regardless of the truth values of inputs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the expression $A \\lor \\neg A,$ and fill out the corresponding truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,\\lnot A\\,$ | $\\,A \\lor \\neg A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n\nAccording to the table, $A \\lor \\neg A$ is always true. Therefore, it is a tautology.\n\nTautologies are represented by the symbol $\\textbf t,$ so we write\n\n$$A \\lor \\neg A = \\textbf t.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "55b05b11-cd0e-5642-b3e7-333b2b27cf0c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9141 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cda6f5a2-c8f2-5cd2-bed0-1aa5c66ed7b7" :step/math-academy-id 158238 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059732 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059445 :question/id #uuid "3b04718a-80ea-53ee-b779-41a3f8ceddc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-14735"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd04f051-39ab-5509-9c41-241a124f58a5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050360}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384877}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Tautologies"} :step/id #uuid "1bf1ba89-729f-56c7-8635-b9544707f81a" :step/math-academy-id 158239 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061009 :tutorial/content "An expression is a **contradiction** if it is always false, regardless of the truth values of inputs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the expression $A \\land \\neg A,$ and fill out the corresponding truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,\\lnot A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land \\neg A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nAccording to the table, $A \\land \\neg A$ is always false. Therefore, it is a contradiction.\n\nContradictions are represented by the symbol $\\textbf c,$ so we write\n\n$$A \\land \\neg A = \\textbf c.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1f28d5b3-bd1c-56f5-92cd-e485de29b331" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9223 :tutorial/title "Contradictions"} :step/id #uuid "081ea716-6cdc-544e-ad1f-83a62c14e0ad" :step/math-academy-id 158242 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059733 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059446 :question/id #uuid "fc9fc6ad-81ff-5416-bae1-287f342512d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-14736"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa71f09b-c241-55c3-87a3-475ee82f586d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412491}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Contradictions"} :step/id #uuid "db18adea-db4b-5653-bd61-8f251aa4a9d0" :step/math-academy-id 158243 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060625}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061002 :tutorial/content "Tautologies have the following properties:\n\n- The disjunction of an expression and a tautology gives a tautology: $$A \\lor \\textbf{t} = \\textbf{t}$$\n- The conjunction of an expression and a tautology gives the initial expression: $$A \\land \\textbf{t} = A$$\n\nContradictions have the following properties:\n\n- The disjunction of an expression and a contradiction gives the initial expression: $$A \\lor \\textbf{c} = A$$\n- The conjunction of an expression and a contradiction gives a contradiction: $$A \\land \\textbf{c} = \\textbf{c}$$\n\nTo prove these properties, we need to fill out the corresponding truth tables. For example, the truth table for the first property is shown below.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\textbf{t}$ | $A \\lor \\textbf{t}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n\n According to the table, $A \\lor \\textbf{t}$ is always true. Therefore, it is a tautology.\n\nFinally, note the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{t}∨\\text{t}=\\text{t}\\, & & \\text{t}∧\\text{t} & =\\text{t} \\\\ \\text{t}∨\\text{c}=\\text{t}\\, & & \\text{t}∧\\text{c} & =\\text{c} \\\\ \\text{c}∨\\text{t}=\\text{t}\\, & & \\text{c}∧\\text{t} & =\\text{c} \\\\ \\text{c}∨\\text{c}=\\text{c}\\, & & \\text{c}∧\\text{c} & =\\text{c}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAgain, all of these properties can be proved using truth tables." :tutorial/id #uuid "9e1edb93-5bcf-5372-87f2-0f30fe620e60" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9147 :tutorial/title "Properties of Tautologies and Contradictions"} :step/id #uuid "b47cae1b-632e-51d7-8a65-07e6d2a66a57" :step/math-academy-id 158246 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059734 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059448 :question/id #uuid "924df88d-5764-5f0d-a808-508dcb8a2b53" :question/math-academy-id "e-14738"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7